      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-79",
              "name": "Reflected XSS via Unsanitized URL in Live Chat Messages",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Reflected XSS via Unsanitized URL in Live Chat Messages"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `linkify` function in `LiveChatTab.tsx` improperly sanitizes user-supplied URLs before inserting them into `<a>` tags within chat messages. This allows attackers to inject arbitrary HTML attributes, leading to reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `linkify` function in `LiveChatTab.tsx` improperly sanitizes user-supplied URLs before inserting them into `<a>` tags within chat messages. This allows attackers to inject arbitrary HTML attributes, leading to reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS).\n\nAn attacker can execute arbitrary JavaScript in the victim's browser, leading to session hijacking, data theft, or defacement.\n\n1. **Sanitize URL before use:** Before constructing an `<a>` tag, ensure that the URL `part` is properly HTML attribute encoded to prevent arbitrary attribute injection. Use a library like `dompurify` or a custom escaping function that specifically targets HTML attribute contexts.\n2. **Validate URL scheme:** Implement strict validation to allow only safe URL schemes (e.g., `http`, `https`, `mailto`) and reject any others, such as `javascript:`, `data:`, or `vbscript:`. While React handles `javascript:` in `href` by default, combining it with attribute injection can still pose risks.\n3. **Use React's built-in escaping for content:** For the text content of the `<a>` tag, ensure it is treated as plain text and not raw HTML, leveraging React's default escaping for JSX interpolated values.",
                "markdown": "The `linkify` function in `LiveChatTab.tsx` improperly sanitizes user-supplied URLs before inserting them into `<a>` tags within chat messages. This allows attackers to inject arbitrary HTML attributes, leading to reflected Cross-Site Scripting (XSS).\n\nAn attacker can execute arbitrary JavaScript in the victim's browser, leading to session hijacking, data theft, or defacement.\n\n1. **Sanitize URL before use:** Before constructing an `<a>` tag, ensure that the URL `part` is properly HTML attribute encoded to prevent arbitrary attribute injection. Use a library like `dompurify` or a custom escaping function that specifically targets HTML attribute contexts.\n2. **Validate URL scheme:** Implement strict validation to allow only safe URL schemes (e.g., `http`, `https`, `mailto`) and reject any others, such as `javascript:`, `data:`, or `vbscript:`. While React handles `javascript:` in `href` by default, combining it with attribute injection can still pose risks.\n3. **Use React's built-in escaping for content:** For the text content of the `<a>` tag, ensure it is treated as plain text and not raw HTML, leveraging React's default escaping for JSX interpolated values."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-798",
              "name": "Hardcoded Secrets in Test Files",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Hardcoded Secrets in Test Files"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Several test files contain hardcoded secrets, such as 'test_app_secret', 'wanderly_signing_secret_2026', and 'skyline_signing_secret_2026'. While these are in test files, it is a security best practice to avoid hardcoding secrets even in non-production code."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Several test files contain hardcoded secrets, such as 'test_app_secret', 'wanderly_signing_secret_2026', and 'skyline_signing_secret_2026'. While these are in test files, it is a security best practice to avoid hardcoding secrets even in non-production code.\n\nPotential information disclosure and increased risk of accidental exposure of sensitive data if test files are ever exposed or mistakenly deployed to a production-like environment.\n\n1. Replace hardcoded secrets with references to environment variables or a dedicated secrets management system.\n2. Ensure that test configurations clearly separate sensitive data from the codebase.\n3. Implement a policy to disallow hardcoding of any secrets, even in test code.",
                "markdown": "Several test files contain hardcoded secrets, such as 'test_app_secret', 'wanderly_signing_secret_2026', and 'skyline_signing_secret_2026'. While these are in test files, it is a security best practice to avoid hardcoding secrets even in non-production code.\n\nPotential information disclosure and increased risk of accidental exposure of sensitive data if test files are ever exposed or mistakenly deployed to a production-like environment.\n\n1. Replace hardcoded secrets with references to environment variables or a dedicated secrets management system.\n2. Ensure that test configurations clearly separate sensitive data from the codebase.\n3. Implement a policy to disallow hardcoding of any secrets, even in test code."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-798",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-835",
              "name": "nanoid (Nano ID) before 5.1.6 contains an infinite loop in the customA ...",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "nanoid (Nano ID) before 5.1.6 contains an infinite loop in the customA ..."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "nanoid (Nano ID) before 5.1.6 contains an infinite loop in the customAlphabet and customRandom functions. When these functions are configured with a size of 0, the internal generation loop never satisfies its exit condition and spins indefinitely, hanging the calling thread. An application that passes an unvalidated, attacker-controlled size of 0 to these functions is exposed to a denial-of-service condition."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "nanoid (Nano ID) before 5.1.6 contains an infinite loop in the customAlphabet and customRandom functions. When these functions are configured with a size of 0, the internal generation loop never satisfies its exit condition and spins indefinitely, hanging the calling thread. An application that passes an unvalidated, attacker-controlled size of 0 to these functions is exposed to a denial-of-service condition.\n\nThe vulnerability in `nanoid` can lead to high impact.\n\nUpgrade `nanoid` to version `3.3.17, 5.1.6` or higher.",
                "markdown": "nanoid (Nano ID) before 5.1.6 contains an infinite loop in the customAlphabet and customRandom functions. When these functions are configured with a size of 0, the internal generation loop never satisfies its exit condition and spins indefinitely, hanging the calling thread. An application that passes an unvalidated, attacker-controlled size of 0 to these functions is exposed to a denial-of-service condition.\n\nThe vulnerability in `nanoid` can lead to high impact.\n\nUpgrade `nanoid` to version `3.3.17, 5.1.6` or higher."
              },
